  "base_commit": "d6b85c00eacbf859f826ee352435eaa03e295509",
  "pr_number": 6189,
  "issue_number": 6188,
  "issue_title": "`ThrowableDeserializer` does not resolve standard `Throwable` property names against `PropertyNamingStrategy`",
  "issue_body": "(generated by Claude code -- apologies for verbosity)\n------\n\n`ThrowableDeserializer` matches the standard `Throwable` property names (\"message\", \"localizedMessage\", \"suppressed\", \"cause\", \"stackTrace\") against hard-coded canonical names using `equalsIgnoreCase()`. That absorbs case-changing renames, but **cannot** match snake- or kebab-cased ones: `\"stack_trace\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"stackTrace\")` is `false`. So under such a `PropertyNamingStrategy` the two multi-word names go unrecognized.\n\nThis is the unfinished half of #3497 (and follow up to #6157), which was closed after fixing only the `cause` part. `ThrowableDeserializer.construct()` still carries the TODO left at the time:\n\n```java\n// 27-May-2022, tatu: TODO -- handle actual renaming of fields to support\n//    strategies like kebab- and snake-case where there are changes beyond\n//    simple upper-/lower-casing\n```\n\nand the read loop repeats the caveat:\n\n```java\n// 26-May-2022, tatu: [databind#3497] To support property naming strategies,\n//    should ideally mangle property names. But for now let's cheat; works\n//    for case-changing although not for kebab/snake cases and \"localizedMessage\"\n```\n\n### Symptoms\n\nAll three reproduced on 3.1 (`SNAKE_CASE`, exception with default + single-String constructors):\n\n**1. `localized_message` is reported as an unknown property.** Harmless with 3.x defaults, but with `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`:\n\n```\nUnrecognizedPropertyException: Unrecognized property \"localized_message\" ...\n  not marked as ignorable (2 known properties: \"stack_trace\", \"cause\")\n```\n\nThe same document with default naming (`localizedMessage`) deserializes fine.\n\n**2. `\"stack_trace\": null` throws.** `_shouldSkipNullValue()` compares against `\"stackTrace\"`, so the null is not skipped and reaches `setStackTrace(null)`:\n\n```\nNullPointerException: Cannot invoke \"[Ljava.lang.StackTraceElement;.clone()\" because \"stackTrace\" is null\n```\n\n**3. A view-filtered `stackTrace` is dropped from input.** `_isStandardThrowableProperty()` exempts the standard properties from `@JsonView` filtering by canonical name, so under `SNAKE_CASE` the exemption misses `stack_trace` and the property is skipped \u2014 the exception keeps its fill-in trace instead of the one from input. With `FAIL_ON_UNEXPECTED_VIEW_PROPERTIES` this becomes a hard `MismatchedInputException` on well-formed round-tripped output.\n\nNote that symptom 3 is a **regression introduced in 3.1.7 by #6174**; symptoms 1 and 2 are long-standing.\n\n`message`, `cause` and `suppressed` are unaffected in practice \u2014 they are single words, so snake/kebab leave them unchanged and `equalsIgnoreCase()` covers `UPPER_CAMEL_CASE`. A *custom* strategy could still rename them, though.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nResolve the five external names once, at construction, rather than comparing canonical names at read time. `ThrowableDeserializer.construct()` already receives the `DeserializationContext` and has a single call site in `BeanDeserializerFactory.buildThrowableDeserializer()`, which can pass the `BeanDescription.Supplier`. Each canonical name can then be run through the naming strategy along with its real accessor \u2014 found reflectively via `beanDesc.findMethod(\"setStackTrace\", ...)`, `findMethod(\"initCause\", ...)` etc. \u2014 exactly the way that method already resolves `cause`:\n\n```java\nname = pts.nameForSetterMethod(config, am, \"cause\");\n```\n\nHanding the strategy the actual member (rather than matching accessor names at read time) also keeps custom strategies that inspect the member working.\n\nI have a working patch against 3.1 doing this \u2014 ~120 lines including tests covering all three symptoms, each verified to fail beforehand. Happy to open a PR against `3.x`.\n\n### Affects\n\n3.1, 3.2, 3.x (2.x not checked). Targeting `3.x` \u2014 too invasive for a patch release.\n",
